  [ 2360ms] limits.maxHistory, as this API reports it: 200
  [ 2360ms] the README's row for the history pager: the Runner stats and history's needs, and the runner has stored more runs than one page holds — the “Runs shown” number, which is the page size (50 by default, or `limits.maxHistory` when that is smaller). It pages the runner's whole history **on the server**, re-reading it with a new offset, and keeps the window in the URL; a run whose log the URL opens is on the page that link carries, and the card says so when it is not on the page shown
  ✓ its row states that figure as a default: the size is the “Runs shown” number, capped by `limits.maxHistory`, and the paging is on the server with the window in the URL
  [ 2360ms] the README's row for `history`: Runs **per page** in the history, sent as `GET /runners/:runner/history?limit=`. Defaults to the smaller of `50` and `limits.maxHistory`. A number outside `1` to `limits.maxHistory` is clamped to that range, not reset. The "Runs shown" select and the pager's own "Rows per page" are two views of it, offering `10`, `25`, `50`, `100` and `200` up to the cap, plus the default and the cap. Choosing the default removes the parameter, and any change resets `offset`.
  ✓ `history` is the page size: the README calls it runs per page, says a change resets `offset`, and the size follows it
  ✓ the figure its pager row states is that default, and the size in force with no `history` in the URL
  [ 2360ms] the README's row for `offset`: Runs skipped before the page, sent as `GET /runners/:runner/history?offset=`. Defaults to `0`. Deliberately uncapped, as the route is: `keepHistory` may store far more runs than `limits.maxHistory`, and this is what reaches them. A value past the end answers an empty page — "No runs on this page", with the pager and Clear history… still live — rather than the last page with rows.
